# DAX Measures — Retail Analytics System

Paste these into Power BI Desktop as new measures on the table noted for
each (Modeling > New Measure). All reference the table/column names
produced by `database/star_schema.sql` — import that as your Power BI
tables (see `POWER_BI_SETUP_GUIDE.md`) before adding these.

Every measure below assumes standard star-schema relationships:
`FactSales[product_key] -> DimProduct[product_key]`, `FactSales[date_key]
-> DimDate[date_key]`, etc. (all single-direction, dimension-to-fact) --
see the setup guide for the exact relationship list.

---

## Sales & Revenue (table: FactSales)

```dax
Total Sales =
SUM ( FactSales[line_revenue] )
```

```dax
Total Discount =
SUM ( FactSales[line_discount_allocated] )
```

```dax
Total Tax Collected =
SUM ( FactSales[line_tax_allocated] )
```

```dax
Net Sales =
[Total Sales] - [Total Discount]
```
Note: line_revenue is already tax-exclusive (Phase 4 stores
selling_price without GST baked in), so "Net Sales" here means
after-discount revenue, not revenue-minus-tax.

```dax
Gross Profit =
SUM ( FactSales[line_profit] )
```

```dax
Profit Margin % =
DIVIDE ( [Gross Profit], [Total Sales], 0 )
```

```dax
Average Order Value =
DIVIDE (
    [Total Sales],
    DISTINCTCOUNT ( FactSales[sale_id] ),
    0
)
```

```dax
Total Orders =
DISTINCTCOUNT ( FactSales[sale_id] )
```

```dax
Units Sold =
SUM ( FactSales[quantity] )
```

## Time Intelligence (requires DimDate marked as the model's official
## Date table — see setup guide)

```dax
Monthly Revenue =
CALCULATE ( [Total Sales], DATESMTD ( DimDate[date_key] ) )
```

```dax
Sales - Previous Month =
CALCULATE ( [Total Sales], DATEADD ( DimDate[date_key], -1, MONTH ) )
```

```dax
Sales Growth % =
DIVIDE (
    [Total Sales] - [Sales - Previous Month],
    [Sales - Previous Month],
    BLANK ()
)
```

```dax
Sales YTD =
TOTALYTD ( [Total Sales], DimDate[date_key] )
```

```dax
Sales Same Period Last Year =
CALCULATE ( [Total Sales], SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR ( DimDate[date_key] ) )
```

## Top Products / Ranking (table: FactSales, use with a Product visual)

```dax
Top Selling Products Rank =
RANKX ( ALL ( DimProduct[product_name] ), [Total Sales], , DESC )
```

```dax
Top 10 Products Sales =
IF ( [Top Selling Products Rank] <= 10, [Total Sales] )
```

## Inventory (table: FactInventory / FactInventory_snapshot)

```dax
Inventory Value =
SUM ( FactInventory[stock_value] )
```

```dax
Low Stock Item Count =
CALCULATE (
    DISTINCTCOUNT ( FactInventory[product_key] ),
    FactInventory[status] = "Low Stock"
)
```

```dax
Out Of Stock Item Count =
CALCULATE (
    DISTINCTCOUNT ( FactInventory[product_key] ),
    FactInventory[status] = "Out Of Stock"
)
```

```dax
Stock Turnover Ratio =
-- Units sold in period / average units held in period. A simple,
-- commonly used approximation -- true turnover needs a stock-movement
-- time series (FactSales gives units sold; FactInventory gives the
-- CURRENT snapshot, used here as the average-stock proxy).
DIVIDE ( [Units Sold], SUM ( FactInventory[current_stock] ), 0 )
```

```dax
Days Of Stock Remaining =
DIVIDE (
    SUM ( FactInventory[current_stock] ),
    DIVIDE ( [Units Sold], 30, 0 ),  -- avg daily sales over the visible period
    BLANK ()
)
```

## Customer Analytics (table: FactSales + DimCustomer)

```dax
Customer Lifetime Value =
CALCULATE (
    [Total Sales],
    ALLEXCEPT ( FactSales, FactSales[customer_key] )
)
```

```dax
Active Customers =
CALCULATE (
    DISTINCTCOUNT ( FactSales[customer_key] ),
    FactSales[status] = "completed"
)
```

```dax
Repeat Customer Rate =
VAR CustomersWithMultipleOrders =
    COUNTROWS (
        FILTER (
            SUMMARIZE ( FactSales, FactSales[customer_key], "Orders", DISTINCTCOUNT ( FactSales[sale_id] ) ),
            [Orders] > 1
        )
    )
RETURN
    DIVIDE ( CustomersWithMultipleOrders, [Active Customers], 0 )
```

## Purchases / Supplier (table: FactPurchases)

```dax
Total Purchase Value =
SUM ( FactPurchases[line_cost] )
```

```dax
Total Units Purchased =
SUM ( FactPurchases[quantity_received] )
```

```dax
Average Unit Cost =
DIVIDE ( [Total Purchase Value], [Total Units Purchased], 0 )
```

## Payments (table: FactPayments)

```dax
Total Payments Collected =
SUM ( FactPayments[amount] )
```

```dax
Cash Payments % =
DIVIDE (
    CALCULATE ( [Total Payments Collected], FactPayments[payment_method] = "cash" ),
    [Total Payments Collected],
    0
)
```

## Employee Performance (table: FactSales + DimEmployee)

```dax
Sales Per Employee =
CALCULATE ( [Total Sales], ALLEXCEPT ( FactSales, FactSales[employee_key] ) )
```

```dax
Orders Handled =
DISTINCTCOUNT ( FactSales[sale_id] )
```

## KPI status measures (for KPI card conditional formatting)

```dax
Sales Growth Status =
VAR g = [Sales Growth %]
RETURN SWITCH ( TRUE (), g >= 0.05, "Good", g >= 0, "Flat", "Down" )
```

```dax
Stock Health Status =
VAR lowCount = [Low Stock Item Count]
RETURN SWITCH ( TRUE (), lowCount = 0, "Healthy", lowCount <= 5, "Watch", "Critical" )
```

---

## Calculated columns (add on the noted table, not as measures)

```dax
-- On DimProduct:
Margin Bucket =
SWITCH (
    TRUE (),
    DimProduct[margin_percent] >= 40, "High Margin",
    DimProduct[margin_percent] >= 20, "Medium Margin",
    "Low Margin"
)
```

```dax
-- On DimCustomer:
Customer Tenure (Years) =
DATEDIFF ( DimCustomer[customer_since], TODAY (), YEAR )
```

## Calculated table example

```dax
-- A standalone "Top 20 Products by Revenue" table for a dedicated page:
Top20Products =
TOPN ( 20, SUMMARIZE ( FactSales, DimProduct[product_name], "Revenue", [Total Sales] ), [Revenue], DESC )
```

---

## DAX optimization notes

- Every measure above uses `DIVIDE()` instead of `/` — avoids
  divide-by-zero errors without needing `IFERROR()`, and is the
  documented best-practice pattern for Power BI performance (`DIVIDE`
  short-circuits; `IFERROR` evaluates the expression first, then
  catches the error, which is slower).
- Time-intelligence measures (`Sales YTD`, `Monthly Revenue`, etc.)
  require `DimDate[date_key]` to be marked as the model's official Date
  table (Table tools > Mark as date table) and related to
  `FactSales[date_key]` — do this once, first, or every time-intelligence
  measure above will error.
- `Customer Lifetime Value` and `Sales Per Employee` use
  `ALLEXCEPT`, which is preferred over `ALL()` + re-adding filters:
  fewer virtual table operations for VertiPaq to materialize.
- Avoid calculated columns where a measure will do (e.g. don't make
  "Total Sales" a calculated column on FactSales) — calculated columns
  are computed at refresh time and stored, and don't respond to slicers;
  measures are computed at query time exactly when needed. The two
  calculated-column examples above are legitimate exceptions: they
  categorize a dimension row (not a fact aggregation), which is exactly
  what calculated columns are for.
